<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How Does the Government Support Communities?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze articles on societal trends. Reading: Construct critical analyses of non-fiction works.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze articles on how government support impacts community services (e.g., "In the article, it says that local programs are funded by the government to improve public infrastructure. This shows that public services are influenced by government priorities.")<br>Can construct critical analyses of non-fiction works on volunteerism (e.g., "The article discusses how the Jóvenes Unidos program helps at-risk youth and suggests that increasing partnerships with local organizations could extend its impact.")<br>Can ask and answer questions on public service articles to explore civic responsibility (e.g., "What have volunteers been doing to improve education?" "Volunteers have been organizing programs and distributing materials to students.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and setting the context for reading about community support.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a headline: "Local Volunteer Brigade Has Been Actively Improving Underserved Areas." Students read the headline and, in pairs, briefly discuss what kind of activities they think the volunteer brigade has been doing and what "underserved areas" might refer to. This activates vocabulary like 'volunteer brigade', 'underserved', 'improve', and the present perfect continuous.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a headline for key information and make predictions. For example, "When I read 'volunteer brigade,' I immediately think of a group of people *dedicating* their time to a *project*. 'Improving underserved areas' suggests positive *impact*." The teacher also models the stress pattern for "volunteer brigade" (vol-un-TEER bri-GADE) and "underserved areas" (un-der-SERVED AR-e-as).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'underserved' a positive or negative word?" (Negative, it means lacking services.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a volunteer brigade 'has been improving' an area, are they finished with the work?" (No, they are still doing it, or it was ongoing until recently.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 0-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "Supporting Our Community" through a non-fiction article. Focus on reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short non-fiction article (approx. 250-300 words) titled "Government Initiatives and Community Well-being." The article discusses how local government *programs are funded* and how *resources are allocated* to *improve public services* and *support* community development. It includes examples of volunteer *involvement* and the *impact* of collaborative *projects*. The register is formal and informative.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding factual texts on societal issues, identifying main ideas and supporting details related to civic responsibility and government support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main idea of the article?" (Students write one sentence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read 3-4 statements and identify if they are true or false based on the article, correcting the false ones. E.g., "Community *programs are funded* solely by private donations." (False) "Volunteers *have been consistently contributing* to various community projects mentioned in the article." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match specific government *initiatives* mentioned in the article to their primary *objective* (e.g., "Youth Development Program" to "Providing *opportunities* for high-risk youth").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 8-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Practicing identifying and understanding target grammar and vocabulary within the context of the article.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying passive voice structures related to support, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous for ongoing community actions. Using vocabulary such as 'government', 'programs', 'resources', 'services', 'volunteers', 'projects', 'influence', 'impact', 'underserved', 'fund', 'allocate', 'improve', 'contribute', 'dedicate', 'engage'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Grammar Spotting):</b> Students re-read the article individually. Their task is to highlight or underline all sentences that use the passive voice (e.g., "Community *programs are funded* by the government," "Decisions *are influenced* by local needs"). They then list three instances where a modal verb expresses necessity or suggestion (e.g., "Individuals *should contribute* to community projects," "More *resources must be allocated*"). Finally, they identify one sentence using the present perfect continuous (e.g., "Volunteers *have been consistently engaging* with residents").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Vocabulary in Context):</b> The teacher provides a list of sentences extracted from the article, with key vocabulary words (e.g., 'allocate', 'impact', 'underserved', 'initiative', 'civic responsibility') removed. Students work in pairs to fill in the blanks using the appropriate word from a provided word bank, ensuring contextual accuracy. E.g., "The government needs to _______ more *resources* to the *underserved* areas." (allocate)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Discussion (Reading for Pragmatic Understanding):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the previous activities. They then discuss two questions related to the article, applying their understanding of the text: "How has the local *government been encouraging* more people to *volunteer* in our *community* according to the article?" and "Why has *community involvement been important* for everyone mentioned in the article?" This targets pragmatic competence by engaging in discussions about civic responsibilities.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 19-30 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language to critically analyze the article within the scenario "Supporting Our Community."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Critical Analysis. Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to construct a critical analysis of the presented article, focusing on the government's role and community *involvement*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a set of guiding questions to help them formulate their analysis:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the main ways community *programs are funded* and *supported by the government* according to the article?" (Passive voice, vocab)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *impact* *has* volunteer *involvement been having* on the success of these *projects*?" (Present perfect continuous, vocab)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Based on the article, what *should individuals do* to further *contribute* to their *community's development*?" (Modals, vocab)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How does the article address the importance of *civic responsibility* and *decision-making*?" (Pragmatic, vocab)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups discuss these questions, sharing their *perspectives* and identifying evidence from the text. They then collaboratively write a short (5-7 sentence) critical analysis summarizing the article's main points, the *influence* of government support, and the role of *volunteers*, incorporating the target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group briefly presents their analysis to another group, fostering sociolinguistic competence by leading collaborative discussions and offering diverse solutions for community involvement.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 31-40 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' ability to read, analyze, and use the target language in their critical analysis. Includes student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a quick review of the written critical analyses. An observation checklist will track group *involvement*, use of target vocabulary, and ability to identify main ideas from the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students’ written critical analysis of the article and their participation in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in analyzing the article's main points about government support and community *services*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'government', 'programs', 'resources', 'volunteers', 'impact', 'fund', 'allocate') and grammatical structures (passive voice, modals, present perfect continuous).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Cohesion and coherence of the written analysis.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active *involvement* and collaboration within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of analyzing the article and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new words or phrases related to community support that you feel confident using now."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 41-47 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the learning process and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has reading about government and volunteer *initiatives* changed your *perspective* on *civic responsibility*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did you use today that helped you understand the article better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading comprehension activities worked well, and students were able to identify the grammar structures. Some groups needed more scaffolding for the critical analysis, specifically in connecting the text directly to their own opinions or proposals. Pronunciation practice on content words was integrated during modeling but could be reinforced more during the practice stage through reading aloud specific sentences. For the next lesson (Speaking), I will ensure opportunities for students to *articulate* their critical analyses orally and engage in debates about government *influence* and *community development*, perhaps by having them propose solutions to *underserved* areas.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 48-50 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>